  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "A number \\(x\\) is \\(2\\) more than the product of its reciprocal and its additive inverse. In which interval does the number lie?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-4 \\le x \\le -2\\)",
    "B": "\\(-2 \\lt x \\le 0\\)",
    "C": "\\(0 \\lt x \\le 2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2 \\lt x \\le 4\\)",
    "E": "\\(4 \\lt x \\le 6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because \\(x\\) has a reciprocal, \\(x\\ne0\\). Its reciprocal is \\(1/x\\), and its additive inverse is \\(-x\\). Their product simplifies to\n\\[\n\\frac1x(-x)=-1.\n\\]\nThe number is two more than this product, so \\(x=-1+2=1\\). Since \\(0<1\\le2\\), the number lies in \\(\\boxed{0<x\\le2}\\), choice C.",
